From e00b04c0bec7de0cac5131cc179506e6bcc756d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bedirhan=20Gen=C3=A7aslan?=
 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 08:45:11 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 42/71] Stop compaction from filtering context the cost model
 decided to keep

The summarization request was billed at full price for the entire history it
was about to discard, because it built a prompt no other request in the session
shares a prefix with. Two things made it different, and neither was a decision
anyone took on purpose.

It sent no tools. An empty tool list is not neutral: the request then omits the
field altogether, so the prompt ahead of the conversation changes and the whole
history re-prefills. Compaction has no step of its own, so it now borrows the
tool specs of the step the turn last executed - the same list the surrounding
requests sent.

It also dropped every reasoning item, including the ones the retention cost
model had already priced as cheaper to carry than to invalidate. Naming no
active turn means "nothing here is still being written", not "re-decide what was
frozen"; honouring the verdict is also what keeps the prefix the verdict paid
for. The verdict is a property of the reasoning, so the caller no longer
overrides it.

Measured on the session both fixes were validated against, the compaction
request costs ~73K effective input tokens with a cold prefix and ~7K with a warm
one, about 13% of that session's bill.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
